# ---------------- Franklin ----------------
meta={
"type":"tale","title_zh":"富兰克林探险队：西北航道上的 129 人","title_en":"The Franklin Expedition: 129 Men and the Northwest Passage",
"era":"1845–1848 年（搜索延续至今）","era_en":"1845–1848 (search continues)",
"region":"加拿大北极群岛，威廉王岛附近","region_en":"Arctic Archipelago, near King William Island, Canada",
"coords":[68.5,-98.8],"tags":["富兰克林","北极","西北航道","探险","考古"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["dyatlov-pass","mh370"],
"en_headings":["Departure and mission","Ice, death, and the loss","Search and Inuit testimony","The wrecks found","What killed them","Heritage and a warming Arctic"],
"limits_zh":"富兰克林探险队的死亡原因没有单一结论。铅中毒、肺结核、坏血病、饥饿、寒冻和同类相食都出现在证据中，但各自的权重仍有争论；早期搜索者的记录、因纽特口述和骨骼分析之间并不总能对齐。沉船发现解决了“船在哪里”，却没有解决“人怎样死”。本文把有物证与档案支撑的时间线写成事实，把死因模型写成并列假说，并注明因纽特证词在叙事中的位置。",
"limits_en":"No single cause explains the deaths of the Franklin expedition. Lead poisoning, tuberculosis, scurvy, starvation, cold, and cannibalism all appear in the evidence, but their relative weight is still argued; early searchers' records, Inuit testimony, and bone analysis do not always align. The discovery of the wrecks answered 'where are the ships' but not 'how did the men die'. This entry treats the timeline supported by artefacts and archives as fact, sets out cause models as competing hypotheses, and marks the place of Inuit testimony in the narrative.",
}
sections=[
("一、出发与目标","1845 年 5 月，英国皇家海军的“幽冥号”（HMS Erebus）与“恐怖号”（HMS Terror）从肯特郡出发，前往北极寻找西北航道。船队由约翰·富兰克林爵士指挥，共 129 人，配备蒸汽机、螺旋桨、大量罐头食品、图书馆和科学仪器。目标是穿过加拿大北极群岛的冰海，打通从大西洋到太平洋的航线。两艘船在格陵兰补给后进入巴芬湾，此后再没有可信的欧洲记录。","In May 1845 the Royal Navy ships HMS Erebus and HMS Terror left Kent for the Arctic to search for the Northwest Passage. The expedition was commanded by Sir John Franklin and numbered 129 men, with steam engines, propellers, large supplies of tinned food, a library, and scientific instruments. The aim was to cross the ice-choked channels of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago and open a route from the Atlantic to the Pacific. After resupplying in Greenland and entering Baffin Bay, the ships left no reliable European record."),
("二、冰、死亡与失踪","1845 年 7 月，捕鲸船在巴芬湾最后一次看到两艘船。此后船队进入兰开斯特海峡，向西进入冰区。1846 年 9 月，船只在威廉王岛附近被浮冰困住；1847 年 6 月 11 日，富兰克林据信去世。1848 年 4 月，幸存者弃船，试图向南步行前往巴克河。写在纸上的“胜利点笔记”记录了这些决定，但笔迹和内容表明，写笔记的人后来也没有到达安全地点。","In July 1845 whaling ships saw the two vessels for the last time in Baffin Bay. The expedition then entered Lancaster Sound and turned west into the ice. In September 1846 the ships were trapped by pack ice near King William Island; on 11 June 1847 Franklin is believed to have died. In April 1848 the survivors abandoned the ships and tried to walk south toward Back River. The written 'Victory Point note' records these decisions, but its handwriting and content indicate that the man who wrote it never reached safety either."),
("三、搜索与因纽特证词","1848 年以后，英国海军和私人搜索队多次进入北极。1850 年代，搜索队在比奇岛发现三名船员墓地，在威廉王岛发现遗物和骨骼；1854 年，约翰·雷从因纽特人那里听到船队最后的消息。因纽特口述记录了“ Aglooka ”（可能指富兰克林或克罗泽）和船员南行的路线，也提到一些令人不安的场景。早期搜索者常把这些证词当作“土著传闻”，但后来的考古发现反复证明其地理细节可靠。","After 1848 the Royal Navy and private search parties entered the Arctic repeatedly. In the 1850s they found three graves on Beechey Island and relics and bones on King William Island; in 1854 John Rae heard from Inuit people the expedition's last news. Inuit testimony recorded 'Aglooka' (possibly Franklin or Crozier) and the crew's southward route, and mentioned disturbing scenes. Early searchers often dismissed this testimony as 'native rumour', but later archaeology has repeatedly confirmed its geographical detail."),
("四、沉船发现","2014 年，加拿大公园管理局的搜索队在威廉王岛附近发现“幽冥号”残骸；2016 年，因纽特向导与搜索队又发现“恐怖号”。两艘船都位于浅水、冰层覆盖的海域，船体结构、舱室和部分器物保存得异常完整。发现解决了长期悬案，也让水下考古成为新的证据来源：舱门、抽屉、仪器和衣物可以显示船员最后时刻的活动，但沉船不等于全部真相。","In 2014 a Parks Canada search team found the wreck of Erebus near King William Island; in 2016 Inuit guides and the search team found Terror. Both lie in shallow, ice-covered water, and their hull structures, cabins, and some contents are remarkably well preserved. The discovery resolved a long-standing question and made underwater archaeology a new evidence source: doors, drawers, instruments, and clothing can show what the crew were doing in their final months, but the wrecks are not the whole truth."),
("五、什么杀死了他们","骨骼上的切割痕迹、锅中的铅、罐头食品的密封工艺、坏血病的牙齿证据和肺结核的病灶，共同构成一幅复杂的死亡图景。铅中毒说认为，罐头食品的铅封和管道中的铅导致慢性中毒，削弱了船员；但并非所有样本都显示高铅，且铅的来源仍有争议。饥饿、寒冻和疾病更直接；在极端条件下，幸存者可能采取同类相食。没有一种因素能单独解释 129 人的结局。","Cut marks on bones, lead in pots, the sealing of tinned food, dental evidence of scurvy, and tuberculosis lesions together form a complex picture of death. The lead-poisoning hypothesis holds that lead solder in tins and pipes caused chronic poisoning and weakened the crews; but not every sample shows high lead, and the source of the lead is still disputed. Starvation, cold, and disease were more immediate; under extreme conditions, survivors may have resorted to cannibalism. No single factor explains the fate of all 129 men."),
("六、遗产与变暖的北极","富兰克林探险队的两艘沉船在 2015 年前后被列为加拿大国家历史遗址，受水下遗产法规保护。气候变化正在改变北极：海冰减少、风暴增强、海水温度上升，既让搜索和考古更容易，也加速木船与遗物的降解。因纽特社区要求在研究、命名和旅游收益中拥有更大发言权。富兰克林的“英雄叙事”正在被重写：它不再只是帝国探险的故事，也是因纽特知识、殖民暴力和环境变化的故事。","The two Franklin wrecks were designated Canadian National Historic Sites around 2015 and are protected under underwater heritage law. Climate change is transforming the Arctic: less sea ice, stronger storms, and warmer water make searching and archaeology easier but also accelerate the decay of wooden hulls and artefacts. Inuit communities are demanding a larger voice in research, naming, and tourism revenue. The 'heroic' Franklin narrative is being rewritten: it is no longer only an imperial exploration story but also a story of Inuit knowledge, colonial violence, and environmental change."),
]
