# ---------------- Bigfoot ----------------
meta={
"type":"creature","title_zh":"大脚怪：脚印、影像与一个未确认的灵长类","title_en":"Bigfoot: Footprints, Film, and an Unconfirmed Primate",
"era":"20 世纪以来（民间叙事更早）","era_en":"20th century onward (older folk narrative)",
"region":"北美，太平洋西北部","region_en":"Pacific Northwest, North America",
"coords":[41.0,-123.7],"tags":["大脚怪","Sasquatch","民间传说","北美","神秘动物学"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["yeti","almas"],
"en_headings":["Name and image","The evidence types","DNA and scientific testing","Film, casts, and hoaxes","Folk roots and culture","Tourism and critical thinking"],
"limits_zh":"大脚怪没有被科学确认，也没有任何身体、骨骼或可重复的 DNA 样本。所谓“证据”主要是脚印、毛发、录音、目击叙述和影像，其中许多可以被熊、人类、已知动物或骗局解释。民间传说、原住民叙事和现代流行文化又相互叠加，不能简单当作生物学记录。本文把“人们讲述了什么”与“是否存在一种未确认的大型灵长类”分开，前者可以研究，后者仍缺证据。",
"limits_en":"Bigfoot is not scientifically confirmed, and there is no body, skeleton, or reproducible DNA sample. The 'evidence' consists mainly of footprints, hair, audio, sightings, and images, many of which can be explained by bears, humans, known animals, or hoaxes. Folk narrative, Indigenous stories, and modern popular culture are layered together and cannot be read simply as biological records. This entry separates 'what people have reported' from 'whether an unconfirmed large primate exists': the first can be studied, the second still lacks evidence.",
}
sections=[
("一、名称与形象","“Bigfoot”这个名字在 1950 年代后期进入美国报纸，源自加州北部发现的大型脚印报道；“Sasquatch”则来自太平洋西北部原住民语言中的类似词。现代形象是一个直立行走、全身覆毛、身高两米以上的类猿生物，出没于森林和山地。这个形象并不是古老的固定传说，而是原住民叙事、边疆故事、马戏怪谈和大众媒体在 20 世纪逐渐合成的结果。","The name 'Bigfoot' entered American newspapers in the late 1950s after reports of large footprints in northern California; 'Sasquatch' comes from a similar word in Pacific Northwest Indigenous languages. The modern image is a bipedal, hair-covered ape-like creature more than two metres tall, living in forests and mountains. This image is not a fixed ancient legend but a twentieth-century composite of Indigenous narratives, frontier stories, carnival tales, and mass media."),
("二、证据的类型","支持者常举出四类材料：脚印与石膏铸模、毛发与组织样本、录音与叫声、目击叙述与照片/影片。问题在于，这些材料大多来自事后提交，缺少可追溯的采集链条；脚印可以伪造，毛发可能来自熊或鹿，录音可能是已知动物或人为，照片可能是模糊、合成或误认。真正有说服力的证据需要身体、骨骼、完整 DNA 或可重复的野外影像，而这些至今没有。","Supporters usually cite four kinds of material: footprints and plaster casts, hair and tissue samples, recordings and calls, and sightings with photos or film. The problem is that most arrive after the fact and lack a traceable chain of collection; footprints can be faked, hair may come from bears or deer, recordings may be known animals or people, and images may be blurry, composite, or misidentified. Persuasive evidence would require a body, skeleton, complete DNA, or repeatable field footage, and none exists."),
("三、DNA 与科学检验","2010 年代，多组研究者对所谓大脚怪毛发和粪便样本做 DNA 分析，结果主要指向熊、狼、鹿、狗和人类，没有发现未知灵长类。2014 年 Sykes 等人的研究分析了数十个样本，结论同样是没有 anomalous primate。也有研究者批评样本来源不严、污染严重。科学检验的关键不是“所有样本都假”，而是“没有一份样本能稳定地指向一种未知的大型灵长类”。","In the 2010s several teams ran DNA analyses on alleged Bigfoot hair and dung samples; results pointed mainly to bears, wolves, deer, dogs, and humans, with no unknown primate. A 2014 study by Sykes and colleagues analysed dozens of samples and likewise found no anomalous primate. Other researchers have criticised loose provenance and contamination. The key point is not that every sample is fake but that no sample stably points to an unknown large primate."),
("四、影像、铸模与骗局","1967 年的 Patterson–Gimlin 影片是最著名的影像：一个直立行走的毛茸生物在加州 Bluff Creek 走过河床。支持者认为它展示了真实的肌肉运动和步态；批评者指出画面模糊、比例可疑，且有多人承认或涉嫌制作骗局。1958 年加州 Humboldt 县的脚印事件，后来被指与 Ray Wallace 的恶作剧有关。骗局的存在不能证明所有报告都假，却说明证据链必须独立于叙述者。","The 1967 Patterson–Gimlin film is the most famous image: a bipedal, hair-covered figure walking across a creek bed at Bluff Creek, California. Supporters see genuine muscle movement and gait; critics note blur, questionable proportions, and people who admitted or were suspected of making hoaxes. The 1958 footprint case in Humboldt County, California, was later linked to Ray Wallace's prank. The existence of hoaxes does not prove every report false, but it shows that the evidence chain must be independent of the narrator."),
("五、民俗与文化的根","大脚怪不只是一个“动物学问题”，也是北美民间文化的一部分。原住民有各自的森林生物叙事，欧洲移民带来了“野人”和“边疆怪物”的想象，20 世纪的大众媒体又把它们合并成 Sasquatch/Bigfoot。目击叙述往往发生在森林、夜间、道路边缘和露营场景，这些地点本身就带有恐惧与神秘感。研究这些叙事，能理解人们如何划分“文明”与“荒野”，而不必先假定生物存在。","Bigfoot is not only a zoological question but part of North American folk culture. Indigenous peoples have their own stories of forest beings; European settlers brought images of 'wild men' and frontier monsters; twentieth-century mass media merged them into Sasquatch/Bigfoot. Sightings often occur in forests, at night, near roads, and around campsites, places already charged with fear and mystery. Studying these narratives can show how people draw the line between 'civilisation' and 'wilderness' without first assuming the creature exists."),
("六、旅游、社群与批判思维","今天的大脚怪围绕旅游、播客、纪录片和商品形成产业：小镇有雕像和节庆，组织有目击数据库，爱好者有年度聚会。对参与者来说，它是一种探险和社群；对科学来说，它仍是一个未确认假说。最有用的态度不是嘲笑，也不是轻信，而是追问证据：样本从哪里来，谁经手，能否重复，是否有独立验证。这个问题比“大脚怪是否存在”更能训练判断力。","Today Bigfoot supports an industry of tourism, podcasts, documentaries, and merchandise: towns have statues and festivals, organisations keep sighting databases, and enthusiasts hold annual gatherings. For participants it is adventure and community; for science it remains an unconfirmed hypothesis. The most useful attitude is neither ridicule nor credulity but a demand for evidence: where did the sample come from, who handled it, can it be repeated, and has it been independently verified? That question trains judgment better than 'does Bigfoot exist'."),
]
